var PEDHD_NEO_DRAFT_B = [

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-12', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-rd',
  stem:'Rebecca was born 48 hours ago at term. She weighed 3.2 kg. You are asked to review her on the postnatal ward as she is breathing very quickly. She is not feeding. On examination she is breathing at 68 breaths/min and has mild chest recession. She looks unwell. You cannot confidently feel her femoral pulses. Her oxygen saturation is 85% in air. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis of that patient?',
  options:['Pneumonia','Transient tachypnoea of the newborn','Coarctation of the aorta','Respiratory distress syndrome','Anaemia'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**A term baby who was well, then collapses at 48 hours with impalpable femoral pulses, is a duct-dependent left heart obstruction until proved otherwise. The tachypnoea is heart failure, not lung disease.**\n\n**Two findings decide it: the timing and the femorals.** `34)Students Obstructive diseases.pptx` describes critical neonatal coarctation exactly as this stem is written — *“Examination on the first day of life is usually normal”*, and *“Neonates usually present with acute circulatory collapse after a few days of age when the duct closes”*, giving *“A sick baby, with severe heart failure”*, *“Absent femoral pulses”* and *“Severe metabolic acidosis”* (slide 50). Rebecca is 48 hours old — the window in which the arterial duct constricts and stops supplying the descending aorta past the narrowing. Her tachypnoea at 68/min with recession is pulmonary venous congestion from a failing left ventricle, her saturation of 85% reflects that congestion plus any residual right-to-left ductal flow, and she *“looks unwell”* and is *“not feeding”* because she is in low cardiac output, not because she has a chest infection. The next steps the lecture teaches are pulse oximetry (slides 52-53), echocardiography for the degree of stenosis and the patency of the PDA (slide 55), and — critically — *“IV prostaglandin infusion”* *“To Keep the duct patent”* (slide 57), with surgical repair as the curative treatment (slide 60). No amount of oxygen fixes this, and giving high-flow oxygen alone can accelerate duct closure.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Pneumonia | Congenital pneumonia is a real cause of neonatal distress and `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` warns it is *“Often indistinguishable from HMD”*, presenting with respiratory distress, hypotension and temperature instability. But infection does not abolish the femoral pulses. If this were sepsis the pulses would be weak and thready everywhere, not selectively absent below the diaphragm |\n| Transient tachypnoea of the newborn | Wrong on timing and on severity. `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` puts the onset *“Within 2 hours after delivery with mild to moderate RD”*, resolving in 3 days, and calls TTN *“a clinical diagnosis”* in a baby who is otherwise well. Rebecca is 48 hours old and looks unwell. This option is the correct answer to `pedhd-neo-13`, which prints the identical five-option menu — the two questions are paired, not duplicated |\n| Respiratory distress syndrome | A disease of prematurity and surfactant deficiency: the same lecture gives an incidence of *“60-80% of <28wk GA”* against *“15-30% of 32-36wk GA”*, with distress appearing *“immediately or shortly after birth”*. Rebecca is term, 3.2 kg, and was well for two days |\n| Anaemia | Anaemia lowers oxygen *content*, not oxygen *saturation* — a profoundly anaemic baby is pale and tachycardic with a normal pulse oximeter reading. It also does not extinguish the femoral pulses |\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A term newborn who was normal at birth and collapses at 24-72 hours with tachypnoea, poor feeding, desaturation and absent femoral pulses has critical coarctation presenting as the duct closes — the femoral pulses, not the chest signs, are what separate a duct-dependent cardiac lesion from every lung cause of neonatal respiratory distress, and the treatment is prostaglandin to reopen the duct.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.58 (Part I, ch.7 Q12)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-13', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-rd',
  stem:'Zak, a full-term male infant, with a birth weight of 3.7 kg, is born by elective caesarean section. His mother was well during pregnancy and had a normal blood glucose screen. Zak becomes tachypnoeic with indrawing between his ribs at 2 hours of age. Examination is otherwise normal. A chest X-ray looks normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis of that patient?',
  options:['Pneumonia','Transient tachypnoea of the newborn','Coarctation of the aorta','Respiratory distress syndrome','Anaemia'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Term, male, elective caesarean, distress at two hours, and nothing else abnormal: this is the textbook risk profile of transient tachypnoea of the newborn, and the stem has been built out of the risk factor list.**\n\n**Every clause is a risk factor or an exclusion.** `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` lists the risk factors for TTN as exactly three — *“Elective CS. Male gender. Maternal Diabetes.”* Zak supplies the first two by name; the third is deliberately withdrawn by telling you his mother *“had a normal blood glucose screen”*, which also removes the infant-of-a-diabetic-mother route to hypoglycaemia and cardiomyopathy. The timing then confirms it: the same lecture gives the onset as *“Within 2 hours after delivery with mild to moderate RD”*, lasting *“from few hours to 3 days”*. The mechanism is mechanical rather than infective or surfactant-related — a *“Mild and transient form of RD resulting from delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid by lymphatics”*, the fluid that a labouring adrenaline surge would normally have switched off and reabsorbed, which is precisely what an elective section before labour prevents. TTN is *“also known as RDS type 2 or wet lung”*, is *“The most common cause of neonatal RD constituting more than 40% of cases”*, and *“Most cases need no active treatment”*, being *“alleviated by supplemental oxygen or CPAP”* and resolving in 3 days.\n\n**The normal chest X-ray is not an argument against TTN.** The lecture states plainly that *“TTN is a clinical diagnosis”*; the classic film shows *“perihilar streaking with fluid in interlobar fissures”*, but the film here is doing a different job — it is excluding the three diagnoses that would have shown something.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Pneumonia | Congenital pneumonia arises from *“aspiration of infected amniotic fluid”* and carries maternal risk factors — fever, prolonged rupture of membranes, foul-smelling liquor (`7) Intro to neonatology.pdf`, `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf`). Zak’s mother *“was well during pregnancy”*, he was delivered by elective section without labour, and the film that would show consolidation is normal |\n| Coarctation of the aorta | This is the answer to `pedhd-neo-12`, which prints the identical option menu; the two are paired, not duplicated. It needs the findings this stem specifically denies — collapse at a few days as the duct closes, absent femoral pulses, desaturation. Zak’s *“Examination is otherwise normal”* |\n| Respiratory distress syndrome | Surfactant deficiency in a preterm lung: synthesis *“begins at 24-28 weeks and is mature by 35 weeks”*, and the radiograph is characteristic — *“a fine reticulogranular ground-glass mottling in both lung fields through which an air bronchogram is seen”*. Zak is full term with a normal film |\n| Anaemia | Produces pallor, tachycardia and poor feeding, not isolated tachypnoea with recession at two hours in a baby who is otherwise normal on examination |\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Tachypnoea and recession beginning within about two hours of an elective caesarean in a well term male infant, with an otherwise normal examination and a normal chest film, is transient tachypnoea of the newborn — delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid, a clinical diagnosis, self-limiting within three days and needing no active treatment in most cases.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.58 (Part I, ch.7 Q13)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-14', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-hie',
  stem:'A 15-month-old girl is referred for developmental assessment. She was born at term weighing 3400 g by spontaneous vaginal delivery and required resuscitation at birth with an Apgar score of 3 at 1 minute, 4 at 5 minutes and 7 at 10 minutes. She was admitted to the neonatal unit and required ventilation for 3 days. On assessment, she has global developmental delay. What is the most likely explanation for this finding?',
  options:['Congenital hypothyroidism','Meningitis','An inborn error of metabolism','Intracranial haemorrhage','Hypoxic–ischaemic encephalopathy (HIE)'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**The Apgar series is the whole question. A term baby whose Apgar is still 4 at five minutes and who needs three days of ventilation has documented neonatal distress, and global delay at 15 months is its expected sequel.**\n\n**Match the stem against the definition.** `12) HIE.pdf` defines the condition as *“Intrapartum Distress or Neonatal Distress”* **plus** *“Neonatal Neurological Symptoms”*, and lists the neonatal distress criteria as *“Depressed Apgar Score at 5 and/or 10 min”*, *“Need for ventilatory support post partum>5 min”*, a cord or first-hour pH below 7.1, and a base deficit over 10 mEq/L. This girl meets the first two outright: her five-minute Apgar is 4, and she was ventilated for 3 days rather than 5 minutes. The same lecture sets the threshold for therapeutic hypothermia at *“Apgar score of ≤ 5 at 5 minutes after birth”* and *“Continued need for resuscitation > 10 minutes”* — she sits inside criterion A, whether or not she was actually cooled.\n\n**The 15-month outcome is the published one.** The lecture reproduces school-age outcomes for moderate and severe HIE: for moderate disease 21% disabled, 12% cerebral palsy, 8% cognitive deficits; for severe disease 100% disabled, 100% cerebral palsy, 82% cognitive deficits and 72% epilepsy. Global developmental delay is the commonest way that injury declares itself in the second year, and `12) HIE.pdf` notes that birth asphyxia accounts for *“23% of all neonatal deaths annually”* — a burden large enough that a perinatal cause should be actively excluded before a rarer one is invented. Note that the Apgar trajectory here *improves* (3 → 4 → 7); a rising score does not undo the insult, it only says resuscitation eventually worked.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Congenital hypothyroidism | It genuinely causes developmental delay — `52) hypothyrodism.pdf` calls it the *“Commonest cause of Preventable Mental Retardation”* — but it is picked up by newborn screening, treated with L-thyroxine, and comes with its own physical signs such as *“Delayed closure of fontanels”*. Nothing in the stem points to it, and a perinatal explanation is already on the page |\n| Meningitis | Neonatal meningitis is an acute illness with its own admission and treatment course; `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` gives it a 21-day antibiotic course and mostly non-specific signs — a tense fontanelle, head retraction. The stem records a resuscitation and ventilation, not an infection |\n| An inborn error of metabolism | `10) new Seizures . Birth injuries , Hpoglycemia 2.pdf` lists IEM among the causes of neonatal seizures, but the classic history is a well interval followed by deterioration with acidosis or hyperammonaemia, often with consanguinity or an affected sibling. None of that is offered, and an IEM does not explain the depressed Apgar at birth |\n| Intracranial haemorrhage | Possible, and it can coexist with asphyxia, but the germinal matrix lesion is a **preterm** one: `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` files IVH and periventricular leukomalacia under prematurity, and `10) new Seizures . Birth injuries , Hpoglycemia 2.pdf` calls IVH *“Especially relevant in preterm infants ≤32 weeks”*. This baby is term at 3400 g |\n\n*(Option E is printed with an en dash in “Hypoxic–ischaemic”, not a hyphen; the spelling is preserved exactly as the book sets it.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Global developmental delay in a term child whose notes record a depressed five-minute Apgar and days of neonatal ventilation is hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy — the diagnosis needs documented perinatal or neonatal distress PLUS neurological signs, and the Apgar and ventilation requirement are the two criteria most often written down.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.58 (Part I, ch.7 Q14; last question printed on p.58)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-15', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-hie',
  stem:'A newborn baby is delivered by caesarean following an obstructed labour. He requires full resuscitation including adrenaline (epinephrine) and his initial capillary blood gas shows a pH of 6.9 (normal range 7.35–7.45) Which of the following is unlikely to be related to the perinatal asphyxia?',
  options:['Hypotension','Renal failure','Necrotizing enterocolitis','Seizures','Heart murmur'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**⚠️ READ THE POLARITY FIRST. This question asks for the ONE finding that is NOT a consequence of the asphyxia. Four options are expected sequelae; the odd one out is the answer.** The word *“unlikely”* is printed in ordinary roman type in the book — not bold, not italic, not underlined — so nothing in the source warns you, and reading it as a normal “which is most likely” question converts a straightforward item into a guaranteed wrong answer. *(The book also omits the full stop after “(normal range 7.35–7.45)”, running the two sentences together, which makes the switch of direction even easier to miss. Both defects are preserved as printed.)*\n\n**How severe this insult is.** `12) HIE.pdf` sets its threshold for neonatal distress at a pH below 7.1 in the cord or the first hour, and its cooling criterion at *“PH < 7.1 within 60 minutes of birth”* with *“Continued need for resuscitation > 10 minutes”*. A pH of 6.9 after full resuscitation including adrenaline is well past both. Obstructed labour belongs with the obstetrical causes the same lecture lists — cord prolapse, placental abruption, rupture of the uterus.\n\n**Why the four related options are related.** Asphyxia is a whole-body ischaemic event, not a cerebral one: cardiac output falls and is redistributed away from the kidneys, gut, muscle and skin to preserve brain and heart, and every organ in that sacrificed group can fail.\n\n| Option | Why it IS related |\n|---|---|\n| Hypotension | Myocardial depression follows severe hypoxia and acidosis, which is why `12) HIE.pdf` puts *“Maintain adequate perfusion”* and *“Vasopressors”* second and third in its supportive management list, immediately after oxygenation and ventilation |\n| Renal failure | Redistribution of cardiac output away from the renal circulation produces acute tubular necrosis, typically with oliguria in the first 24-48 hours, and it is the commonest non-neurological organ failure after birth asphyxia *(not taken from the course material — the multi-organ consequences of asphyxia are not described in the cached lectures)* |\n| Necrotizing enterocolitis | Gut ischaemia is one of the accepted triggers: `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` states NEC’s *“Etiology uncertain, but may involve ischemia, mucosal insult, infection”*, and the same lecture lists NEC among the complications of the growth-restricted, chronically hypoxic infant |\n| Seizures | The single most expected sequel. `10) new Seizures . Birth injuries , Hpoglycemia 2.pdf` puts hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy at the head of its aetiology list at *“(40%)”*, notes seizures on *“day 0-3 might be related to perinatal asphyxia”*, and calls HIE the *“Most common acute symptomatic cause in term and near-term infants”*, with onset *“in the first 12–24 hours”*. Seizures are also a Sarnat staging item in `12) HIE.pdf` |\n\n**Why “Heart murmur” is the answer.** A murmur in a newborn points at cardiac *structure* — a septal defect, an obstructive lesion, a persistent duct — and structure is laid down in the first trimester, long before an intrapartum event. Nothing about a hypoxic labour creates a structural lesion. The word chosen is *“unlikely”* rather than “never”, and that is the right word: severe asphyxia can transiently produce a murmur through papillary muscle ischaemia with tricuspid regurgitation, or through a duct that stays open in a sick, acidotic baby *(not taken from the course material)*. But as a **causal** consequence of this labour, a murmur is the outlier, and it is the intended key.\n\n*(The book prints “Necrotizing” with a z here and again in `pedhd-neo-17`, in a text that is otherwise British; the spelling is preserved as printed.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Severe perinatal asphyxia is a multi-organ ischaemic insult — hypotension, acute renal failure, necrotising enterocolitis and seizures all follow from redistributed cardiac output and cerebral injury — whereas a heart murmur reflects cardiac structure formed in early gestation and is the one listed finding the labour cannot have caused; and the stem is negatively worded with no typographic warning.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.59 (Part I, ch.7 Q15)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-16', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-rd',
  stem:'A male infant born at 29 weeks is now 4 hours old. He has developed an increased oxygen requirement, there is tachypnoea and severe intercostal and subcostal recessions. CXR demonstrates a ground glass appearance. The most likely diagnosis is which of the following?',
  options:['Transient tachypnoea of the newborn','Respiratory distress syndrome','Congenital pneumonia','Pneumothorax','Congenital heart disease'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Twenty-nine weeks, four hours old, and a ground-glass film: three separate pointers to surfactant deficiency, and the film is the one that closes it.**\n\n**Gestation.** `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` states that *“The incidence of RDS is inversely proportional to gestational age”*, giving *“60-80% of <28wk GA”* against *“15-30% of 32-36wk GA”*. The reason is stated directly: *“Surfactant synthesis begins at 24-28 weeks and is mature by 35 weeks of gestation”*, and surfactant — *“a lipoprotein material”* secreted by type II alveolar cells — is what stops the alveoli collapsing on expiration. At 29 weeks this baby is at the steep end of that curve.\n\n**Timing.** `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` gives RDS type I an *“Onset of respiratory distress 1st 4 hours”*, which is exactly when this infant declared himself; `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` adds that the course *“aggravates rapidly with progressive respiratory distress and cyanosis in the first 48 hours”*, matching the rising oxygen requirement.\n\n**The film.** Both lectures describe the same appearance — *“small lung volume”* with *“a fine reticulogranular ground-glass mottling in both lung fields through which an air bronchogram is seen”*. Note that the bank describes this film in words but prints no radiograph for the question; the appearance has to be recognised from the phrase alone. Treatment is supportive care, surfactant and assisted ventilation, with antenatal betamethasone or dexamethasone given 48 hours before delivery at 34 weeks or less as prevention.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Transient tachypnoea of the newborn | Wrong population and wrong film: TTN *“occurs in term and late preterm neonates”*, is mild and transient, and shows *“perihilar streaking with fluid in interlobar fissures (wet lung)”*, not diffuse ground glass. Its risk factor is elective caesarean, not extreme prematurity |\n| Congenital pneumonia | The strongest distractor, and honestly so — `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` says congenital pneumonia is *“Often indistinguishable from HMD”* clinically. What separates them here is the radiograph: pneumonia gives consolidation (the lecture illustrates *“Right upper lobar consolidation”*), and the stem offers no maternal risk factor — no fever, no prolonged rupture of membranes, no foul-smelling liquor |\n| Pneumothorax | Presents as *“Sudden deterioration of respiratory status”* with *“Decreased breath sounds and chest movement in affected side”* and a chest bulge — an **asymmetrical**, abrupt event, usually complicating mechanical ventilation. This baby has bilateral ground glass, not a unilateral lucency |\n| Congenital heart disease | Would be expected to declare itself with cyanosis unresponsive to oxygen, a murmur, or absent femoral pulses, and typically later than four hours, as the duct closes. It does not produce a bilateral reticulogranular film |\n\n*(The stem is a comma splice as printed — “an increased oxygen requirement, there is tachypnoea” — preserved exactly as the book sets it.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A preterm infant of 29 weeks who develops rising oxygen needs, tachypnoea and severe recessions within the first four hours, with a bilateral fine reticulogranular ground-glass film and air bronchograms, has respiratory distress syndrome from surfactant deficiency — incidence rises steeply as gestation falls, because surfactant is not mature until about 35 weeks.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.59 (Part I, ch.7 Q16)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-17', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal',
  stem:'A female infant born at 28 weeks is now 10 days old. She had been doing very well and breast milk was started 2 days ago via nasogastric tube. She has become increasingly unwell over the past 12 hours with abdominal distension, temperature instability and bile stained aspirates from the NG tube. Some blood is noted in the stools. AXR shows air within the bowel walls. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Sepsis','Intestinal obstruction','Necrotizing enterocolitis','Malrotation','Duodenal atresia'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Air within the bowel wall is pneumatosis intestinalis, and pneumatosis in a fed preterm infant is necrotising enterocolitis. Nothing else on this list puts gas inside the bowel wall.**\n\n**The radiological sign is the diagnosis.** `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` names it explicitly under NEC — *“pneumatosis intestinalis”* — and describes the entity as *“Most common serious GI problem in the preterm infant”*, with an *“Etiology uncertain, but may involve ischemia, mucosal insult, infection”* and an *“Incidence inversely related to GA”*. At 28 weeks this baby sits in the highest-risk band. The gas is produced by bacteria fermenting substrate in an ischaemic, damaged mucosa, so all three limbs of that aetiology — ischaemia, mucosal insult and infection — leave the same footprint on the film. Note that the bank describes the abdominal radiograph in words but prints no film for this question.\n\n**Every other clause is a NEC clause.** The trigger is enteral feeding started two days ago; the presentation is the GI list the same lecture gives — *“Excessive gastric residual”*, *“Emesis”*, *“Abdominal distention”*, *“Abdominal tenderness”* — with bile-stained aspirates showing the stasis has reached beyond the pylorus, blood in the stools showing mucosal breakdown, and temperature instability showing the systemic inflammatory response. Day 10 is the classic window: late enough for feeds to have been established, early enough for the preterm gut still to be vulnerable. Management is to stop feeds, decompress the stomach, and cover anaerobes — `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` specifies *“Suspected anerobic infection (NEC).. Metronidazole or clindamycin”*.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Sepsis | The closest call, and the two overlap constantly — `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` gives late-onset sepsis exactly these non-specific signs (*“Hypo/hyperthermia”*, feed intolerance, vomiting, poor feeding), and NEC is very often accompanied by bacteraemia. What decides it is the pneumatosis: septicaemia alone does not put gas in the bowel wall. The same lecture lists *“Abdominal catastrophes like volvulus”* in the differential of late-onset sepsis for this reason |\n| Intestinal obstruction | Would give distension and bilious aspirates, but the film shows dilated loops and fluid levels, not intramural gas, and mechanical obstruction does not cause blood in the stools or temperature instability |\n| Malrotation | The important surgical differential of a bilious aspirate — `24)Abdominal pain.pdf` flags *“Bilious vomiting”* and *“Volvulus”*, and `22)Vomiting.pdf` lists malrotation among the surgical emergencies. But midgut volvulus is an abrupt, catastrophic obstruction with a normal or gasless abdomen on plain film, not a 12-hour illness with pneumatosis, and it has no relationship to the day feeds were started |\n| Duodenal atresia | A congenital atresia declares itself with bilious vomiting in the first day or two of life and a “double bubble” — the baby never establishes feeds at all. This infant *“had been doing very well”* for eight days. It is also the anomaly associated with trisomy 21, listed among the features of Down syndrome in `6) Genetically determined disease_.pdf` |\n\n*(The book prints “Necrotizing” with a z here as in `pedhd-neo-15`, and “bile stained” unhyphenated; both are preserved as printed.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A preterm infant who deteriorates within days of starting enteral feeds with abdominal distension, bilious aspirates, bloody stools and temperature instability has necrotising enterocolitis, and pneumatosis intestinalis — air within the bowel wall on the abdominal film — is the sign that separates it from sepsis, obstruction and the surgical causes of bilious vomiting.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.59 (Part I, ch.7 Q17)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-18', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal-sepsis',
  stem:'A term female baby is seen on the postnatal ward. She is 18 hours old and has not been feeding well. Her mother was told she needed to have antibiotics prior to labour but delivered too quickly to receive them. She is admitted to the neonatal unit with suspected sepsis and CXR confirms congenital pneumonia.  What is the most likely causative organism?',
  options:['Streptococcus pneumoniae','Escherichia coli','Listeria monocytogenes','Group B haemolytic streptococcus','Chlamydia pneumoniae'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The clue is the antibiotics that were never given. A mother told she needs antibiotics *prior to labour* is a mother screened positive for group B streptococcus, and the baby who is delivered before the prophylaxis can be given is the baby who gets early-onset GBS disease.**\n\n**Timing puts it in the early-onset window.** `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` divides neonatal sepsis at 72 hours: early-onset sepsis occurs in the *“first 72 hours of life”*, is transmitted vertically, presents as *“Asymptomatic at birth, pneumonia or septicemia”*, is driven by *“Maternal Risk factors”*, and is caused by *“GBS and Echoli”*. This baby is 18 hours old and has congenital pneumonia — early-onset, textbook.\n\n**The numbers behind intrapartum prophylaxis.** The same lecture states that *“Around 15%–40% of pregnant women have rectal or vaginal colonization of group B streptococci ; about 50% of these infants become colonized and 1%–2% of the colonized infants develop early-onset infection”*, and lists the risk factors that trigger prophylaxis: preterm prolonged rupture of membranes, *“prolonged (>18 hours) or prelabour rupture of the membranes”*, *“intrapartum fever >38°C or chorioamnionitis”*, a previous child with GBS infection, and GBS bacteriuria in pregnancy. `30) Pneumonia.pdf` is more direct still: for a *“Neonate younger than 3 weeks”*, *“Infection is through vertical transmission from the mother during birth”* with *“Group B Streptococci”* named first, and its prevention slide names *“testing and treating pregnant women for Group B Streptococci”*. The route to the lung is aspiration: `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` gives congenital pneumonia the pathogenesis *“aspiration of infected amniotic fluid”* with the *“Same organisms as in neonatal sepsis”*, and warns it is *“Often indistinguishable from HMD”*. Empirical cover is ampicillin plus gentamicin or amikacin for septicaemia and pneumonia.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Streptococcus pneumoniae | A pathogen of infants and children past the neonatal period — `30) Pneumonia.pdf` places it in the 3 months to 5 years, 5-10 years and 10-21 years bands. It is not a maternal genital coloniser and has no relation to intrapartum prophylaxis |\n| Escherichia coli | Genuinely the *second* organism of early-onset sepsis, named in the same breath as GBS by `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` and listed for 2-14 day pneumonia by `30) Pneumonia.pdf`. It is the better answer in a preterm or very low birth weight infant, and it dominates neonatal meningitis. But the maternal antibiotic story in this stem is specifically GBS prophylaxis, and GBS is the commoner early-onset organism at term |\n| Listeria monocytogenes | A real neonatal pathogen — `18) febrile child and life threatening infections.pdf` lists it with GBS and E. coli for neonatal-to-3-month meningitis, and `30) Pneumonia.pdf` includes *“L.monocytogens”* in the 2-14 day list. It is far rarer, and its story is a maternal food-borne illness with a flu-like prodrome, not a screening swab |\n| Chlamydia pneumoniae | The wrong Chlamydia and the wrong age. `30) Pneumonia.pdf` puts *“Chlamydia pneumoniae”* in the **10-21 years** band; the neonatal species is *Chlamydia trachomatis*, acquired at delivery, causing conjunctivitis and an afebrile pneumonia at several weeks of age, and it is the one the same lecture names in its newborn prevention slide |\n\n*(Option D is printed “haemolytic” with the diphthong, while option E of `pedhd-neo-8` on p.56 prints “hemolytic” without it. The book is inconsistent with itself and both spellings are preserved as printed. The double space after “congenital pneumonia.” in the stem is likewise the book’s own.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Congenital pneumonia in the first 72 hours of life is early-onset sepsis acquired vertically, and group B streptococcus is the leading organism at term — a mother who was screened positive and delivered too fast to receive intrapartum prophylaxis names the pathogen outright, with E. coli the main alternative and Listeria a distant third.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.59 (Part I, ch.7 Q18; the question straddles the page break, with the end of the stem, all five options and the answer printed at the top of p.60)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-19', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haem-bleeding',
  stem:'A term infant born by home delivery is brought to accident and emergency on day 4. His mother has noticed a small amount of bleeding from the umbilical stump, which is still oozing some blood at present. His mother mentions that her other children received vitamin K in the hospital but she cannot recall her newborn receiving this. Which of the following blood results is likely to be abnormal?',
  options:['Platelets','Prothrombin time (PT)','Activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT)','Haemoglobin','Antiplatelet antibodies'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**A home delivery, no vitamin K, and umbilical oozing on day 4 is classic vitamin K deficiency bleeding. The test that goes abnormal first, and most, is the prothrombin time.**\n\n**The disease.** `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .pdf` files *“Hgic disease of newborn”* among the **acquired** coagulation disorders, alongside DIC, liver disease and anticoagulants — a factor deficiency, not a platelet or vessel problem. Vitamin K is the cofactor for the gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX and X; the newborn starts with low stores, gets little in breast milk, and has no gut flora to make more, which is why a single intramuscular dose is given at birth. Classic vitamin K deficiency bleeding appears on days 2-7 — day 4 here — and umbilical stump oozing is one of its signature sites.\n\n**Why PT rather than APTT.** Factor VII has by far the shortest half-life of the four vitamin K-dependent factors, and it is the factor the extrinsic pathway depends on. The prothrombin time is therefore the first test to prolong and the most sensitive marker of the deficiency, which is why the bank keys it *(the relative half-lives of the vitamin K-dependent factors are not stated in the cached lectures — not taken from the course material)*. `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .pdf` gives the reference ranges as *“PT (12-14 sec)”* and *“PTT (24-40 sec)”*.\n\n**⚠️ A divergence worth knowing about, recorded and not corrected.** The same lecture prints a DIC-versus-vitamin-K-deficiency comparison table, and in the vitamin K deficiency column it marks **both** *“PTT”* and *“PT”* as prolonged, with bleeding time, thrombin time, platelet count and fibrinogen all normal and FDPs negative. On that table option C is abnormal too, and the printed key of B is defensible only as the *earliest and most sensitive* abnormality rather than the *only* one. The book prints a bare letter with no option name and no explanation, so nothing in the source resolves the ambiguity; the answer here follows the printed key. In practice both are reported as prolonged in established disease, and a normal platelet count with a prolonged PT is the combination that separates this from DIC.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Platelets | Explicitly normal in the lecture’s comparison table (*“Platelet count … n”*), which is exactly how vitamin K deficiency is told apart from DIC, where the count falls sharply and burr and helmet cells appear. A low count would instead point at immune or alloimmune thrombocytopenia |\n| Activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT) | See the divergence above — it does prolong, but later and less sensitively. It is the *isolated* abnormality of haemophilia A, which the same lecture describes in a male newborn with *“Newborn bleeding [ umbilicus, circumcision, I.C Hge ]”* and lab findings of *“PTT prolonged”* with *“PT normal”*. That is the differential this stem defuses with the missing vitamin K, not with the coagulation screen |\n| Haemoglobin | A consequence of how much blood has been lost, not of the defect itself. The stem describes *“a small amount of bleeding”* still *“oozing”*, which will not have dropped the haemoglobin measurably at four days |\n| Antiplatelet antibodies | The investigation for immune thrombocytopenic purpura, defined in the same lecture as *“Autoimmune destruction of platelets by IgG”*. It tests a mechanism this baby does not have, and it would be paired with a low platelet count, which vitamin K deficiency does not cause |\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bleeding from the umbilical stump on day 2-7 in a baby who missed vitamin K prophylaxis is vitamin K deficiency bleeding, an acquired deficiency of factors II, VII, IX and X: the prothrombin time is the first and most sensitive test to prolong, the platelet count stays normal — which is what separates it from DIC — and an isolated prolonged APTT would point instead at haemophilia.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.60 (Part I, ch.7 Q19)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-20', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'liver',
  stem:'A 4-week-old formula fed girl presents with jaundice. Her mother reports that her urine looks dark and her stools are pale. She has failed to regain her birth weight. Her total bilirubin is 135 (normal <100 mmol/L) the conjugated fraction is 65 (normal <20 mmol/L). Her full blood count and reticulocytes are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Physiological jaundice','Biliary atresia','Spherocytosis','G6PD deficiency','Urinary tract infection'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Pale stools plus dark urine plus a conjugated fraction that is half the total is obstructed bile flow, and in the first months of life the commonest cause of obstructed bile flow is biliary atresia. This is a surgical emergency measured in weeks.**\n\n**⚠️ THE UNITS ARE WRONG IN THE BOOK, AND THEY ARE WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS OF THIS QUESTION.** The bank prints *“(normal <100 mmol/L)”* and *“(normal <20 mmol/L)”*. **The correct unit is µmol/L — micromoles, not millimoles.** A bilirubin of 135 mmol/L is not a high result, it is a physically impossible one: it would be roughly 79,000 mg/dL, a thousandfold beyond anything compatible with life or with blood as a liquid. The values themselves are entirely ordinary in µmol/L — 135 µmol/L is about 7.9 mg/dL total, with a conjugated fraction of 65 µmol/L, about 3.8 mg/dL. The same book prints the micro sign correctly for the same analyte elsewhere in the same chapter, at *“150 µmol/L”* and *“250 µmol/L”* in `pedhd-neo-8` and *“170 µmol/L”* in `pedhd-neo-10`, both on pp.56-57, so it contradicts itself two pages earlier. The box was re-rendered at 400 dpi specifically to exclude a scanning artefact and the glyph is an unambiguous plain “m”. **The stem above is left exactly as the book prints it and the key does not move; read “mmol/L” as “µmol/L” throughout.** For completeness: the cached text of `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` also reads “mmol/L” in five places — *“5 mg/dL (86 mmol/L)”*, *“15 mg/dL (258 mmol/L)”*, *“12 mg/dL (206 mmol/L)”* and so on — where the mg/dL conversions printed beside them prove micromoles (5 mg/dL ≈ 86 µmol/L). Whether the slide itself misprints it or the text extraction dropped the glyph cannot be settled without re-rendering the slide, so that lecture is not offered here as proof of anything beyond the arithmetic.\n\n**Now the medicine.** `43)Infantile cholestasis_.pdf` defines neonatal cholestasis as *“a conjugated bilirubin level (>1) mg/dL (or (>20%) of total serum bilirubin)”*. Here the conjugated fraction is 65 of 135 — **48%**, more than double the threshold. The lecture then hands over the exact clinical triad in the stem: cholestasis *“presents with jaundice (beyond 2 weeks), hepatomegaly, and pathognomonic pale (clay-colored) stools with dark urine (bilirubinuria)”*, adding that acholic stools are *“a critical marker of severe bile flow obstruction”* and that *“Conjugated bilirubinuria makes the diaper yellow or brown”*. It states outright that *“The most common cause of cholestatic jaundice in the first months of life is biliary atresia (up to 60%)”* and that *“Any infant who has jaundice after age 2 weeks should be evaluated for cholestasis”*. The failure to regain birth weight fits: without bile in the gut, fat and fat-soluble vitamins are not absorbed.\n\n**Why the clock matters.** The same lecture insists the diagnostic approach must identify biliary atresia *“immediately, ideally within the first 6 weeks of life”*, that liver biopsy is *“Highly recommended before 6 weeks of age”*, and that *“Early Kasai portoenterostomy (KPE) is critical for BA survival before 6 weeks of age”*. This girl is four weeks old. Investigation runs total and direct bilirubin, liver enzymes, GGT and coagulation, then abdominal ultrasound looking for a *“Small gall bladder, or the ‘triangular cord sign’”*, then percutaneous liver biopsy (*“Highly accurate (96.9%)”*), with operative cholangiography as *“the gold standard”*.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Physiological jaundice | Excluded twice over. It is **unconjugated**, and `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` confines it to a peak at *“3–5 days of age”* with visible jaundice resolving *“by 1 week in the fullterm infant and by 2 weeks”* in the preterm. A conjugated fraction of 48% at four weeks is not physiological under any definition; `43)Infantile cholestasis_.pdf` states that cholestatic jaundice *“is always pathologic”* |\n| Spherocytosis | A haemolytic cause of **unconjugated** hyperbilirubinaemia. `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` groups haemolysis under overproduction with the *“reticulocyte count elevated”*; this girl’s full blood count and reticulocytes are explicitly normal, and haemolysis darkens the stools rather than bleaching them |\n| G6PD deficiency | Same objection. It is an overproduction, unconjugated mechanism — the lecture notes female heterozygotes are at risk through X-chromosome inactivation, so being a girl does not exclude it, but normal reticulocytes and a conjugated fraction do |\n| Urinary tract infection | The most respectable distractor: `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` lists UTI as presenting with *“Vomiting , fever, prolonged jaundice , poor weight gain”*, which covers two of this stem’s clauses. What it cannot explain is the pale stool. UTI does not obstruct the biliary tree, and acholic stools are the sign that sends the infant to surgery rather than to a urine culture |\n\n*(The book also omits the full stop after “(normal <100 mmol/L)”, running the next clause straight on, and prints “formula fed” unhyphenated; both are preserved as printed.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Jaundice persisting beyond two weeks with pale stools, dark urine, poor weight gain and a conjugated fraction above 20% of the total is neonatal cholestasis, and biliary atresia is its commonest cause — the diagnosis must be made and Kasai portoenterostomy performed before six weeks of age, and the bank prints the bilirubin units as mmol/L where it can only mean µmol/L.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.60 (Part I, ch.7 Q20)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-21', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal',
  stem:'A 6-day-old baby who is breast feeding well and thriving is referred by the midwife because she appears jaundiced. She has pigmented stools and is passing urine normally. Examination is unremarkable. Her bilirubin is 68 (normal <100 mmol/L) with a conjugated level of 8 (normal <20 mmol/L).Which of the following would be your management of this infant?',
  options:['Complete a prolonged jaundice screen','Commence phototherapy','Top up feeds with formula milk','Repeat the bilirubin level in 8 hours','No treatment required'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Both numbers are below the normal limits the question itself supplies. This is a visibly jaundiced but biochemically normal, thriving six-day-old — physiological jaundice, and the correct management of physiological jaundice is to do nothing.**\n\n**⚠️ THE UNITS ARE WRONG IN THE BOOK, THE SAME ERROR AS `pedhd-neo-20`.** The bank prints *“(normal <100 mmol/L)”* and *“(normal <20 mmol/L)”*; **the analyte can only be measured in µmol/L — micromoles, not millimoles.** Millimolar bilirubin is a physical impossibility, and the same book prints the micro sign correctly for bilirubin two pages earlier, at *“150 µmol/L”* and *“250 µmol/L”* in `pedhd-neo-8` and *“170 µmol/L”* in `pedhd-neo-10`. The box was re-rendered at 400 dpi to exclude a scanning artefact and the glyph is an unambiguous plain “m” in both brackets. **The stem stays exactly as the book prints it and the key does not move; read “mmol/L” as “µmol/L”.** In µmol/L the figures are unremarkable: a total of 68 µmol/L is about 4 mg/dL, and a conjugated level of 8 µmol/L is about 0.5 mg/dL.\n\n**Read the two numbers against the two thresholds.** The total, 68, is below the quoted upper limit of 100. The conjugated level, 8, is below 20 — and as a fraction it is 12% of the total, under the 20% that `43)Infantile cholestasis_.pdf` uses to define cholestasis (*“a conjugated bilirubin level (>1) mg/dL (or (>20%) of total serum bilirubin)”*). So there is no conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, and the clinical picture agrees: *“pigmented stools”* mean bile is reaching the gut, and normal urine means no bilirubinuria — the exact opposite of the pale stools and dark urine the same lecture calls *“pathognomonic”* of obstruction.\n\n**And the timing is ordinary.** `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` describes physiological jaundice as visible jaundice appearing after 24 hours, rising by less than 5 mg/dL per day, peaking at *“3–5 days of age”* and resolving by one week in a term infant. It also notes that 65% of newborns become visibly jaundiced in the first week. A thriving, well-examined six-day-old sits squarely inside that description. For scale, the same lecture puts the risk of bilirubin encephalopathy as *“small in healthy, term neonates even at bilirubin levels of 25–30 mg/dL”* — some six times this baby’s level.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Complete a prolonged jaundice screen | The right investigation at the wrong time. `43)Infantile cholestasis_.pdf` triggers it at a defined point — *“Any infant who has jaundice after age 2 weeks should be evaluated for cholestasis including with total and direct bilirubin levels”* — and this baby is six days old. Her fractionated bilirubin has in any case already been done and is normal |\n| Commence phototherapy | Nothing to treat. `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` starts phototherapy *“electively when the TSB is approximately 6 mg/dL … lower than the predicted exchange level for that infant”*, using the AAP gestation- and age-specific charts; at about 4 mg/dL on day 6 at term she is nowhere near any threshold. Phototherapy is safe but not free — it separates mother and baby, needs eye shielding *“to prevent retinal damage”*, and would medicalise a normal newborn |\n| Top up feeds with formula milk | This is the treatment for breast-feeding-associated jaundice, where the mechanism is *“poor enteral intake and increased enterohepatic circulation”* and the lecture advises supplemental formula *“If intake is inadequate”*, judged on *“the infant’s weight, urine, and stool output”*. This baby is *“breast feeding well and thriving”*, so the premise fails. Introducing formula unnecessarily undermines an established feed |\n| Repeat the bilirubin level in 8 hours | Serial measurement is for a level close to the threshold or rising steeply, or for jaundice within the first 24 hours, which the same lecture treats as pathological until proved otherwise. Her level is low, her trajectory is past the day 3-5 peak, and she is well; an eight-hour recheck buys nothing and repeats a heel prick |\n\n*(The book prints no space after the full stop — “mmol/L).Which of the following” — and prints “breast feeding” as two unhyphenated words; both are preserved as printed.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A thriving term newborn in the first week with visible jaundice, pigmented stools, normal urine, a total bilirubin below the phototherapy threshold and a conjugated fraction under 20% has physiological jaundice and needs no treatment at all — a prolonged jaundice screen belongs after two weeks, formula top-ups belong only where intake is inadequate, and the bank again prints the bilirubin units as mmol/L where it can only mean µmol/L.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.60 (Part I, ch.7 Q21; last question of the chapter, straddling the page break with all five options and the answer printed at the top of p.61)'
}

];
